    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>This <strong>FitDesk 2.0 review</strong> covers the FitDesk FDX 2.0, a folding exercise bike with a built-in desk surface. Instead of putting a pedal machine under your existing desk, you sit on the bike and use its own work surface for a laptop or tablet. It is a different approach from an under desk elliptical, and it suits a different kind of buyer.</p>
<p>Based on specs and owner reviews, the FitDesk is a good pick if you want a real seated cardio session while you read, answer email or watch a lecture. It is less ideal if you want to keep working at your normal desk, in your normal chair, for hours at a time. Below we cover what owners like, the downsides, and how it compares with the best under desk ellipticals.</p>

<h2>What Is the FitDesk FDX 2.0?</h2>
<p>The FitDesk FDX 2.0 is a desk bike. It has a seat, pedals with magnetic resistance, a digital display that tracks basic stats, and a small desk surface in front of you where handlebars would normally be. The X-shaped frame folds, so you can roll it aside when you are done.</p>
<p>People often search for a "fit desk elliptical," but the FitDesk is a bike, with a circular pedal stroke. That distinction matters for comfort and for how you will use it, which is why we compare it with ellipticals throughout this review.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Quick verdict:</strong> the FitDesk is a pedal workstation, not an under desk accessory. Buy it if you are happy to move to it for focused sessions. Buy an under desk elliptical if you want to keep moving at your own desk all day.</p></div>

<h2>FitDesk 2.0 Review: What Owners Like</h2>
<h3>A real cardio option</h3>
<p>Because you sit upright on a proper bike with your weight over the pedals, it is easier to work hard than on a seated under desk elliptical. Owners who want to raise their heart rate during the workday tend to rate it well. Moderate cycling effort is clearly more demanding than the light pedaling most people do under a desk.</p>
<h3>Everything in one unit</h3>
<p>You do not need a desk at the right height or a chair that does not roll. The FitDesk brings both. That is useful in a small apartment, a spare room, or anywhere you lack a proper workstation.</p>
<h3>It folds away</h3>
<p>The folding frame lets you tuck it against a wall. For a machine of this type, owners appreciate being able to reclaim the floor space at the end of the day.</p>
<h3>Quiet enough for home use</h3>
<p>Magnetic resistance keeps it quieter than friction bikes. It is generally fine for a home office, though not as silent as the best seated ellipticals.</p>

<h2>FitDesk Reviews: The Common Complaints</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Seat comfort.</strong> Like most exercise bikes, the saddle is not an office chair. Long sessions can get uncomfortable, and some owners add a gel seat cover.</li>
<li><strong>Small work surface.</strong> It fits a laptop and not much else. Two monitors, a mouse pad and paperwork will not fit.</li>
<li><strong>Typing while pedaling.</strong> Light, steady pedaling works. Hard effort makes accurate typing and mouse work difficult.</li>
<li><strong>Footprint.</strong> Even folded, it takes more space than an under desk machine.</li>
<li><strong>Not for long workdays.</strong> Most owners use it for sessions, not as their main desk for eight hours.</li>
</ul>
<p>Check the current listing for the user height range and seat adjustment, since fit is the biggest factor in whether a bike feels comfortable. Very tall or very short riders should read owner comments from people of a similar height before ordering, because a bike that does not fit will be the first thing you stop using.</p>

<h2>FitDesk 2.0 vs Under Desk Ellipticals</h2>
<p>This is the choice most people researching FitDesk reviews are actually making. Both let you move while you work, but in very different ways.</p>
